1
00:00:03,878 --> 00:00:07,631
Ma, Ma, calm down. Listen to me.
I know it says click with the mouse...

2
00:00:07,798 --> 00:00:11,092
...but on a laptop,
the track pad is the mouse.

3
00:00:11,260 --> 00:00:15,760
Now, put your finger on it.
No, doesn't matter which finger.

4
00:00:16,557 --> 00:00:17,599
[SCOFFS]

5
00:00:17,767 --> 00:00:19,185
Good choice.

6
00:00:19,352 --> 00:00:22,146
Yeah, now move it down
to your e-mail icon.

7
00:00:22,313 --> 00:00:24,857
Yeah, the little envelope.

8
00:00:25,024 --> 00:00:28,527
What do you mean what does it
look like? It looks like an envelope!

9
00:00:28,694 --> 00:00:31,655
Fine, you don't like the computer,
don't use it.

10
00:00:31,822 --> 00:00:35,492
Sure, we can exchange it
for a salad spinner. Goodbye.

11
00:00:37,286 --> 00:00:38,287
[SIGHS]

12
00:00:38,454 --> 00:00:41,915
That's a Hanukkah present
you're regretting, huh?

13
00:00:42,083 --> 00:00:46,170
Yeah, I won't say that all senior citizens
who can't master technology...

14
00:00:46,337 --> 00:00:50,174
...should be publicly flogged, but if
we made an example of one or two...

15
00:00:50,341 --> 00:00:52,843
...It might give the others
incentive to try harder.

16
00:00:54,804 --> 00:00:57,598
I had a great idea.
You know how we're always having...

17
00:00:57,807 --> 00:00:59,934
...to stop
and solve differential equations...

18
00:01:00,101 --> 00:01:03,646
...like when you do Fourier analysis
or using the Schrödinger equation?

19
00:01:03,813 --> 00:01:05,648
Howard doesn't. He's only an engineer.

20
00:01:07,441 --> 00:01:11,653
I think we could write a little app that
would use handwriting recognition...

21
00:01:11,821 --> 00:01:14,323
...then run it through
a symbolic evaluation engine.

22
00:01:14,490 --> 00:01:17,910
You just use your smartphone,
take a picture of the equation, and bam!

23
00:01:18,077 --> 00:01:22,577
You know what's a great app?
The one that makes fart noises.

24
00:01:27,169 --> 00:01:31,669
You know, Leonard,
that's actually a valid idea. Very good.

25
00:01:32,717 --> 00:01:35,386
Can you say that
and not make it sound like I'm a cat. ..

26
00:01:35,553 --> 00:01:38,097
~who learned how to use the toilet?

27
00:01:38,723 --> 00:01:41,892
No. The two achievements
are equally surprising...

28
00:01:42,059 --> 00:01:43,393
...and equally admirable.

29
00:01:43,561 --> 00:01:46,939
Though if pressed,
I'd have to give a slight edge to the cat.

30
00:01:47,106 --> 00:01:50,776
I'll save you the pain and nervousness
of waiting for the answer.

31
00:01:50,943 --> 00:01:54,404
I agree to be part of your project.
Congratulations.

32
00:01:54,864 --> 00:01:56,490
Ha, ha. Yay for me.

33
00:01:57,867 --> 00:01:59,910
What about you?
I can't promise anything...

34
00:02:00,077 --> 00:02:02,579
...but people do make money
off stuff like this.

35
00:02:02,747 --> 00:02:04,290
A few extra bucks would be nice.

36
00:02:04,457 --> 00:02:07,293
-Could move out of my mother's house.
-Where would you go?

37
00:02:07,460 --> 00:02:11,714
I always dreamed about building
a little place of my own over the garage.

38
00:02:12,423 --> 00:02:14,800
You know, if I made more money,
I could take time off. ..

39
00:02:14,967 --> 00:02:17,135
...and give pretty girls
submarine rides.

40
00:02:17,720 --> 00:02:20,723
What's that, some weird sex thing?

41
00:02:21,599 --> 00:02:24,393
No. You take girls underwater
in your private submarine...

42
00:02:24,560 --> 00:02:26,228
...and you show them fish.

43
00:02:26,812 --> 00:02:28,980
Why does everything
have to be dirty with you?

44
00:02:30,149 --> 00:02:33,652
I think we could work on this at night
and maybe in a couple of weeks...

45
00:02:33,819 --> 00:02:37,280
-...we'll have ourselves an app to sell.
-Sounds like we're in business.

46
00:02:37,448 --> 00:02:41,452
- I think we should capture this moment.
-Yeah, let's do it.

47
00:02:42,787 --> 00:02:44,205
[FART NOISE]

48
00:02:46,582 --> 00:02:50,252
Was that the best 99 cents I ever spent
or what?

49
00:03:15,361 --> 00:03:16,612
I checked the App Store.

50
00:03:16,779 --> 00:03:20,282
No one is doing handwriting recognition
differential equation solving.

51
00:03:20,449 --> 00:03:22,951
Oh, dear Lord, shush.

52
00:03:23,953 --> 00:03:27,331
-What?
-You have so few good ideas, Leonard.

53
00:03:27,498 --> 00:03:31,627
And you're just gonna spill the beans
in front of an outsider?

54
00:03:37,299 --> 00:03:38,883
Who, me?

55
00:03:39,093 --> 00:03:43,013
Sheldon, I don't think Penny cares
about handwriting-recognition based--

56
00:03:43,180 --> 00:03:45,974
[SINGSONG]
The stars at night are big and bright

57
00:03:46,142 --> 00:03:48,811
Deep in the heart of Texas

58
00:03:49,019 --> 00:03:51,688
-She's not going to steal our idea.
“What idea?

59
00:03:51,897 --> 00:03:53,773
-We're gonna write--
-[SINGSONG] The Prairie sky

60
00:03:53,941 --> 00:03:55,067
Is wide and high

61
00:03:55,234 --> 00:03:56,485
Deep in the heart--

62
00:03:56,652 --> 00:03:59,738
-Okay, stop, stop.
-Of Texas

63
00:04:00,489 --> 00:04:03,700
Whatever your secret is,
I'm sure it's boring. So I'm not interested.

64
00:04:03,868 --> 00:04:07,371
Well, it's not boring at all.
You see, in higher-order mathematics...

65
00:04:07,538 --> 00:04:09,873
-...there are certain sets of equations--
-Bored.

66
00:04:10,916 --> 00:04:12,709
-Satisfied?
-[IN NORMAL VOICE] Hardly.

67
00:04:12,877 --> 00:04:15,838
Consider this unlikely
but very plausible scenario.

68
00:04:16,005 --> 00:04:18,674
A young woman alone in the big city.

69
00:04:18,841 --> 00:04:21,009
Her ridiculous dream
of becoming an actress...

70
00:04:21,177 --> 00:04:23,262
lies shattered about her.

71
00:04:23,429 --> 00:04:26,473
-Hey, wait a minute.
-Hang on, let's see where he's going.

72
00:04:26,640 --> 00:04:29,476
Then it hits her,
how is she going to survive?

73
00:04:29,643 --> 00:04:33,188
But she has no prospects,
no marketable skills.

74
00:04:33,355 --> 00:04:36,566
And then one day,
she meets a group of geniuses...

75
00:04:36,734 --> 00:04:38,527
_and their friend Howard.

76
00:04:41,864 --> 00:04:44,366
-Hey, |-
-Hang on, let's see where he's going.

77
00:04:45,534 --> 00:04:50,034
She befriends them, and then lies in wait
until they reveal a marketable idea...

78
00:04:50,915 --> 00:04:53,876
...which she steals
and sells to the highest bidder.

79
00:04:54,043 --> 00:04:55,127
That is ridiculous.

80
00:04:55,294 --> 00:04:57,754
Is it? Let's see you come up
with an explanation...

81
00:04:57,922 --> 00:05:01,091
...as to why this woman
hangs out with us all the time.

82
00:05:04,720 --> 00:05:06,805
I've already mooched dinner
off you guys.

83
00:05:06,972 --> 00:05:11,059
-I don't need to listen to this.
-There's your answer, free food.

84
00:05:12,228 --> 00:05:15,898
All right, I've taken the liberty
of drafting these workflow charts...

85
00:05:16,148 --> 00:05:18,900
...which outline our duties
and the path we will follow...

86
00:05:19,068 --> 00:05:21,987
...as we develop
our groundbreaking new app.

87
00:05:22,780 --> 00:05:25,365
Hey. Why am I in charge
of phone support?

88
00:05:27,701 --> 00:05:29,703
Seems a bit racist.

89
00:05:29,870 --> 00:05:32,664
A customer service representative
with an Indian accent...

90
00:05:32,832 --> 00:05:35,501
...will create the impression
we're a vast enterprise...

91
00:05:35,668 --> 00:05:37,461
_that uses overseas call centers.

92
00:05:37,628 --> 00:05:41,548
Oh, very clever. But still racist.

93
00:05:41,715 --> 00:05:45,552
Duly noted, Steve from Wichita.

94
00:05:45,719 --> 00:05:49,264
Hey, why am I listed
as your executive assistant?

95
00:05:49,431 --> 00:05:53,017
Because the word secretary
has fallen into disrepute.

96
00:05:53,185 --> 00:05:55,270
Oh, FYI,
my mother's birthday is coming up.

97
00:05:55,437 --> 00:05:57,480
I'm going to need you
to pick up a present.

98
00:05:58,899 --> 00:06:01,109
Okay, we need to stop for a minute.

99
00:06:01,277 --> 00:06:04,113
We can talk during break.
We have one coming up at midnight.

100
00:06:04,280 --> 00:06:07,658
-And we'll need snacks.
-Sheldon, you're not in charge.

101
00:06:07,825 --> 00:06:10,160
-It's my idea. I'm in charge.
-Oh, of course, you are.

102
00:06:10,327 --> 00:06:13,538
Look at the organizational chart.
You're clearly listed as founder.

103
00:06:13,747 --> 00:06:17,333
Yes, and you're listed
as chief executive officer. ..

104
00:06:17,501 --> 00:06:20,462
...chief financial officer
and chief operating officer.

105
00:06:20,629 --> 00:06:23,632
You missed chief science officer,
chairman of the board and. ..

106
00:06:23,799 --> 00:06:27,052
-...head of the Secret Santa committee.
-Okay.

107
00:06:27,261 --> 00:06:31,761
You need to get clear on this right now:
I am in charge of this project.

108
00:06:32,558 --> 00:06:36,311
Ooh, Leonard's going all alpha nerd
on Sheldon's ass.

109
00:06:37,313 --> 00:06:39,315
All right.
I'm not going to argue with you.

110
00:06:39,481 --> 00:06:41,733
Sheldon Cooper
is nothing if not a team player.

111
00:06:41,901 --> 00:06:45,362
Dr. Hofstadter, you have the con.

112
00:06:46,655 --> 00:06:49,532
Thank you. I thought we might start...

113
00:06:49,700 --> 00:06:52,869
-...by talking about the user interface.
-Dear Lord, we're doomed.

114
00:06:55,414 --> 00:06:59,000
So this button here
will allow you to scan a new equation...

115
00:06:59,168 --> 00:07:01,003
...but over here, you can press this...

116
00:07:01,170 --> 00:07:03,881
...and just substitute
new values for the coefficients.

117
00:07:04,048 --> 00:07:05,924
Ha, ha. Good one, boss.

118
00:07:09,595 --> 00:07:11,430
-What?
-This diagram.

119
00:07:11,597 --> 00:07:13,724
Well, I assume
you were opening with a joke.

120
00:07:13,891 --> 00:07:18,186
It certainly buoyed up this employee's
esprit de corps.

121
00:07:19,605 --> 00:07:21,690
It's not a joke. It's the real design.

122
00:07:21,857 --> 00:07:26,357
In that case, may I offer 27 little tweaks
to make it slightly less embarrassing?

123
00:07:27,529 --> 00:07:30,615
Sheldon, I think this will work.
Let's just try it my way.

124
00:07:30,824 --> 00:07:33,034
Oh, I'm sorry.
I assumed that you wanted...

125
00:07:33,202 --> 00:07:35,245
...candid truth-telling
from your employees.

126
00:07:35,412 --> 00:07:37,956
But I realize now,
you want the mindless nattering...

127
00:07:38,123 --> 00:07:40,542
...of complacent yes-men.

128
00:07:41,126 --> 00:07:43,545
-Are you done?
-If you say so, boss.

129
00:07:46,048 --> 00:07:49,551
Right now, this button-press event
s triggering a dummy procedure call.

130
00:07:49,718 --> 00:07:51,761
How about this for the app name?

131
00:07:51,929 --> 00:07:55,223
The Surprisingly Helpful
Equation-Linked Differential...

132
00:07:55,391 --> 00:07:58,060
...Optimized Numerator.

133
00:07:58,894 --> 00:08:01,438
So it spells "Sheldon."

134
00:08:02,731 --> 00:08:05,900
Does it? A happy accident.

135
00:08:06,735 --> 00:08:10,280
-We're not doing names now. Howard?
-Like I was saying...

136
00:08:10,447 --> 00:08:13,575
...now, it's a dummy procedure.
Eventually, it will drop us in--

137
00:08:13,742 --> 00:08:15,577
If we're not going to give it a name...

138
00:08:15,744 --> 00:08:18,496
...can we at least give it
a secret code designation?

139
00:08:18,664 --> 00:08:19,748
-No.
-Hear me out.

140
00:08:19,915 --> 00:08:22,167
The atomic bomb
was the Manhattan Project...

141
00:08:22,376 --> 00:08:25,003
...Windows 95
was originally Project Chicago.

142
00:08:25,170 --> 00:08:29,507
For our app, I would humbly suggest
Project Nodlehs.

143
00:08:30,467 --> 00:08:32,802
That's just Sheldon backwards.

144
00:08:33,012 --> 00:08:34,930
Another happy accident.

145
00:08:35,889 --> 00:08:38,600
We're not wasting time
with names right now.

146
00:08:38,767 --> 00:08:40,477
I see.

147
00:08:40,894 --> 00:08:43,438
-Is that decision open for discussion?
-No.

148
00:08:43,981 --> 00:08:45,607
Is the decision to not discuss..

149
00:08:45,774 --> 00:08:47,442
-...open for discussion?
-No.

150
00:08:47,985 --> 00:08:49,611
Again, right now...

151
00:08:49,778 --> 00:08:52,655
...It's a dummy procedure,
but eventually, it will drop us--

152
00:08:52,823 --> 00:08:55,826
I call for a vote of no confidence
and a change in leadership.

153
00:08:55,993 --> 00:08:58,286
-Sheldon, stop it.
“When in the course of human events. ..

154
00:08:58,454 --> 00:09:00,789
...It becomes necessary
to dissolve the bonds. ..

155
00:09:00,956 --> 00:09:03,500
-...that tie three scientists--
-Enough, Sheldon.

156
00:09:03,667 --> 00:09:05,627
Let's get to the vote. Show of hands...

157
00:09:05,794 --> 00:09:08,296
...all opposed to Leonard
and his reign of tyranny:

158
00:09:11,133 --> 00:09:14,970
-Okay, that's it, you're fired.
-Really?

159
00:09:15,137 --> 00:09:16,138
Why?

160
00:09:17,931 --> 00:09:20,558
Because you're impossible
to work with.

161
00:09:21,143 --> 00:09:22,144
I see.

162
00:09:26,190 --> 00:09:28,192
And is that open for discussion?

163
00:09:34,156 --> 00:09:37,868
-What are you doing?
-Oh, good morning, shoemaker.

164
00:09:38,035 --> 00:09:42,205
I think you'll be pleased with what
the elves were up to last night.

165
00:09:42,873 --> 00:09:44,207
But I fired you.

166
00:09:44,374 --> 00:09:46,459
Oh, I know.
I'm now an independent contractor.

167
00:09:47,878 --> 00:09:49,921
No, you are now nothing.

168
00:09:50,089 --> 00:09:52,841
You have no connection
to this project whatsoever.

169
00:09:53,008 --> 00:09:54,884
But I made it better.

170
00:09:55,052 --> 00:09:57,512
I don't want it better. I want it my way.

171
00:09:57,679 --> 00:10:00,056
Well, that speaks volumes, doesn't it?

172
00:10:01,183 --> 00:10:04,853
All right, I suppose
I'll go put on my bus pants.

173
00:10:05,020 --> 00:10:07,939
-What the hell are bus pants?
-They are pants one wears...

174
00:10:08,107 --> 00:10:10,692
...over regular pants
when one sits on bus seats..

175
00:10:10,859 --> 00:10:13,194
...that other people
have previously sat on.

176
00:10:14,404 --> 00:10:17,698
But, perhaps, from your lofty heights
atop the corporate ladder...

177
00:10:17,866 --> 00:10:21,870
...you've lost touch with the struggles
of the common man.

178
00:10:22,037 --> 00:10:24,622
Look, I'm still happy
to drive you to work.

179
00:10:24,790 --> 00:10:28,585
Nothing's changed in that regard.
We're still roommates, still friends.

180
00:10:28,752 --> 00:10:31,546
Except you identified me,
your best friend...

181
00:10:31,713 --> 00:10:33,047
...as a gangrenous limb...

182
00:10:33,215 --> 00:10:36,051
...that needed to be severed
from the organism and tossed.

183
00:10:36,218 --> 00:10:40,718
The desperate act of a little man
with a big Napoleon complex.

184
00:10:43,517 --> 00:10:46,269
You know what?
Go put on your bus pants.

185
00:10:53,443 --> 00:10:57,071
Mm, last night, I had a dream
we got so rich from the app...

186
00:10:57,239 --> 00:10:59,866
...you and I bought
matching side-by-side mansions.

187
00:11:00,033 --> 00:11:01,075
[CHUCKLES]

188
00:11:01,243 --> 00:11:02,869
But there was a secret tunnel...

189
00:11:03,036 --> 00:11:06,080
...connecting your front yard
to my back yard.

190
00:11:11,086 --> 00:11:12,504
What do you think that means?

191
00:11:14,047 --> 00:11:17,592
It means after we play handball,
I'm showering at home.

192
00:11:20,721 --> 00:11:23,890
Oh, good morning,
friend Howard, friend Ra.

193
00:11:24,057 --> 00:11:26,934
I see you gentlemen
are enjoying beverages.

194
00:11:27,102 --> 00:11:30,105
Perhaps, they would taste better
out of these.

195
00:11:31,732 --> 00:11:35,944
-"World's Greatest Astrophysicist"?
-Don't thank me, you earned it.

196
00:11:37,446 --> 00:11:40,073
"Howard Wolowitz."

197
00:11:42,284 --> 00:11:44,661
Why not "World's Greatest Engineer"?

198
00:11:44,828 --> 00:11:47,330
Oh, I'm sorry,
I typed that into the label maker...

199
00:11:47,539 --> 00:11:51,125
...put I just couldn't press enter.
Now down to business.

200
00:11:51,293 --> 00:11:53,795
What would it take
for you to abandon Leonard...

201
00:11:53,962 --> 00:11:55,797
...and join me in a rival company?

202
00:11:55,964 --> 00:11:57,966
Unless, of course, mugs are sufficient.

203
00:11:58,133 --> 00:12:00,468
In which case, welcome aboard.

204
00:12:01,053 --> 00:12:05,348
-We're not quitting on Leonard.
-I understand. Your loyalty is admirable.

205
00:12:05,557 --> 00:12:08,977
But what if I were to up the ante?

206
00:12:09,811 --> 00:12:11,145
What are you talking about?

207
00:12:11,772 --> 00:12:16,272
Monogrammed key chains
with a built-in laser pointer.

208
00:12:19,988 --> 00:12:20,989
No?

209
00:12:22,449 --> 00:12:26,949
How about can cozies
emblazoned with our university mascot?

210
00:12:28,455 --> 00:12:30,331
Go Beavers.

211
00:12:32,834 --> 00:12:34,460
I'll be back.

212
00:12:36,171 --> 00:12:38,339
Okay, let's try this one.

213
00:12:41,051 --> 00:12:42,719
Spherical Hankel function.

214
00:12:42,886 --> 00:12:44,554
Hold on.

215
00:12:44,763 --> 00:12:46,848
-That's it!
-Yes! Eureka!

216
00:12:47,015 --> 00:12:50,685
We agreed when it was "eureka” time,
we were all gonna say it together.

217
00:12:50,978 --> 00:12:54,773
-Fine, let's say it together.
-No, the moment has passed.

218
00:12:55,732 --> 00:12:57,692
Greetings, gentlemen.

219
00:12:57,859 --> 00:12:59,569
How goes your little project?

220
00:12:59,736 --> 00:13:02,363
You mean Project Lenwoloppali?

221
00:13:03,740 --> 00:13:06,909
A combination of all your names.
Very clever.

222
00:13:07,119 --> 00:13:09,704
I assume "Koothranardowitz"
was taken.

223
00:13:11,623 --> 00:13:14,042
We're scanning equations
and getting good results.

224
00:13:14,209 --> 00:13:16,544
Oh, well, then by all means, carry on.

225
00:13:16,712 --> 00:13:18,880
I wouldn't wanna
impede your progress.

226
00:13:19,047 --> 00:13:21,424
Howard, did you solve
the install time problem?

227
00:13:21,633 --> 00:13:24,177
No, it's tricky. I'm gonna try having it
pick up the libraries--

228
00:13:24,344 --> 00:13:26,804
[PLAYING THEREMIN]

229
00:13:32,394 --> 00:13:33,770
What are you doing?

230
00:13:34,938 --> 00:13:37,398
Playing the theremin.

231
00:13:39,026 --> 00:13:42,404
No, I mean,
what are you doing with a theremin?

232
00:13:43,238 --> 00:13:44,906
Playing it.

233
00:13:45,907 --> 00:13:49,911
I've loved the theremin from the moment
I heard original Star Trek theme.

234
00:13:50,078 --> 00:13:54,578
And it's been killing me that
it just sits in my closet gathering dust.

235
00:14:08,805 --> 00:14:12,391
